---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
import { breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';

const jsonLd = breadcrumbJsonLd([
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Standards', path: '/standards' },
]);

const updated = '2026-10-02';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial standards"
  description="Signal Daily's AI-use disclosure, sourcing rules, confidence levels, corrections policy and independence statement."
  path="/standards"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <header class="sr-page-head">
    <p class="sr-micro sr-micro--accent">Standards · updated {updated}</p>
    <h1>Editorial standards</h1>
    <p class="sr-page-head__lede">
      These rules apply to news editions and standalone writing, with distinct requirements for each format. If an item cannot meet them, it does not publish.
      Where we fall short, we correct in place and note the change.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="sr-prose">
    <h2>1. AI use is disclosed</h2>
    <p>
      News editions use AI-assisted drafting and an automated publishing workflow. Manual writing may
      use AI assistance, while the named author is responsible for its interpretation. Human editorial
      review and automated validation are distinct activities.
      Concretely:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>An AI model drafts structure and prose from collected source material.</li>
      <li>
        Editors may review, rewrite or reject news and manual writing. Automated checks validate
        structure and citations; they do not themselves constitute human editorial review.
      </li>
      <li>
        AI assistance does not establish evidence. References and attribution remain required wherever
        factual claims or direct quotations need support.
      </li>
      <li>
        AI is never used to generate quotes, invent sources, or synthesize a source that does not
        exist. If a claim has no source, the claim does not ship.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Primary sources only</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Every news body paragraph cites at least one source, and every citation resolves to a real,
        publicly reachable URL in the item’s source list.
      </li>
      <li>
        Preference order: the original document (filing, paper, transcript, dataset, official
        statement), then direct reporting by a newsroom that did original work, then secondary
        aggregation — which is used only for discovery and never cited as the basis of a claim.
      </li>
      <li>
        Press releases and social posts are labelled as such where they are the source of record, and
        are never presented as independent confirmation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Cluster coverage exists so readers can compare how multiple outlets framed the same event. It
        is not an endorsement of any of them.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Authored writing</h3>
    <p>Standalone essays, features and analysis identify their author and publication date. Direct quotations from classics, historical explanations and factual claims need references. Personal experience and interpretation are attributed to the author. Free-form writing does not require news paragraph citations, three summary bullets or related-report clusters.</p>

    <h2>3. News confidence labels</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>High</strong> — the core claim is documented by two or more independent primary
        sources, or by one authoritative source with no credible dispute.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Medium</strong> — a single credible source, or sources that agree on the outline but
        not the details.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Low</strong> — developing, contested, or based on a source with a track record we
        cannot yet assess. Treated as provisional and edited as the record firms up.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For news, confidence describes the state of the evidence, not the importance of the story. A low-confidence
      item can be the most consequential thing in the edition.
    </p>

    <h2>4. No advice</h2>
    <p>
      Nothing on Signal Daily is investment, legal, medical or professional advice. We do not publish
      recommendations, price targets, trading signals, dosing guidance or clinical advice. Items that
      touch on markets or health describe what happened and what is documented — not what anyone
      should do about it.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Corrections</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Factual errors are corrected in place, with the edition page updated and the correction noted
        in the item’s editor’s note. Standalone writing records a meaningful correction in its text
        and updates its modification date.
      </li>
      <li>
        If an item cannot be corrected to standard, it is removed from the archive and the removal is
        noted on the edition page.
      </li>
      <li>
        Citation rot is treated as a defect: when a source link dies, we replace it with an archive
        copy or remove the claim it supported.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>6. Independence, privacy and access</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No sponsored content, no affiliate links, no paid placements in the digest.</li>
      <li>No accounts, no login walls, no email capture, no analytics or advertising scripts.</li>
      <li>
        Source-reference links carry <code>rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow"</code> and open in a new tab.
      </li>
      <li>
        The only data stored in your browser is your theme and view preference, kept in{' '}
        <code>localStorage</code> on your device. It is never transmitted.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>7. Scope</h2>
    <p>
      Six categories: <strong>AI</strong>, <strong>BIO</strong>, <strong>GEO</strong>,
      <strong>MARKETS</strong>, <strong>WISDOM</strong> and <strong>SOCIETY</strong>.
      Automated collection covers the first four; WISDOM and SOCIETY begin with manual publication. An item
      is included when it is documented, material to a reader who follows the field, and adds
      information rather than repeating the previous day’s coverage.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about these standards, or a correction request, can be raised against the published
      site at <a href={SITE.url}>{SITE.url.replace(/^https?:\/\//, '')}</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
